WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.127
The headline finding is striking.

00:00:02.207 --> 00:00:08.749
Plotting rollout reward against training step
reveals dramatic swings between consecutive iterates,

00:00:08.829 --> 00:00:12.963
and zooming in shows a jagged, almost fractal
reward landscape,

00:00:13.043 --> 00:00:16.776
all while the behavior cloning loss sits flat and smooth.

00:00:16.856 --> 00:00:21.190
That jaggedness is completely invisible in the
one-step objective.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.933
Now apply the exponential moving average,

00:00:24.013 --> 00:00:29.551
and those oscillations are dramatically damped,
across architectures, dataset sizes,

00:00:29.631 --> 00:00:34.835
and multiple tasks, with no change to the learning
rate schedule or batch size.

00:00:34.915 --> 00:00:38.982
And the same butterfly effect shows up in language
generation,

00:00:39.062 --> 00:00:46.073
where two nearly identical training checkpoints
produce stories that diverge into totally different plots.

00:00:46.153 --> 00:00:52.360
Here too the moving average tames the instability
and even yields the lowest-perplexity model.
